  "prompt": "How do the stages of life shape our understanding of time and mortality?",
  "answer": "Our sense of time changes as we move through life. In childhood, time often feels abundant and elastic: a summer can seem endless, and the future is mostly possibility. As adults, routines, responsibilities, and repeated experiences can make years feel as if they pass faster. In later life, attention may turn more often to memory, legacy, and the time that remains.\n\nMortality gives these shifts weight. Early on, death may feel distant or abstract. With age, loss and bodily change can make it more immediate, prompting us to reconsider what deserves our attention. That awareness can bring anxiety, but also clarity: time feels more valuable not simply because there may be less of it, but because we understand better what we want to do with it.\n\nThese are patterns, not rules. A crisis, a new relationship, grief, illness, or a change in circumstance can alter someone’s sense of time at any age. Often, the deepest shift is from seeing time as something we have to spend to seeing it as the life we are already living.",
